From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] rpcrt4: NdrConvert and NdrConvert2: calls from machines with another data representation (3312)

CORE review: RPC (DECISIONS-2 RPC-NDR).

A client or server of another architecture sends its data in its own
representation, named by the format label of the packet: integers big-endian,
characters EBCDIC, floating point numbers in IEEE, VAX F/G, IBM or Cray format.
rpcrt4 left such data alone (NdrConvert was an empty stub that logged a FIXME).

New ndr_convert.c: NdrConvert2 walks the type format strings the way the
unmarshallers read the buffer (base types, pointers and their deferred
referents, full pointer ids, structures of every kind, arrays, strings, unions,
context handles, ranges, [pipe] chunks) and converts the data in place; NdrConvert
does the same for the parameter list of the original interpreter format. The
stubs call NdrConvert2, and a server then answers in the local representation.

The fields of request, response and fault headers are in the byte order of the
label, so they are swapped when received from, and when sent in, a big-endian
representation; a client takes the representation of a response from its header.
---

diff --git a/dlls/rpcrt4/ndr_convert.c b/dlls/rpcrt4/ndr_convert.c
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/dlls/rpcrt4/ndr_convert.c
@@ -0,0 +1,1398 @@
+/*
+ * NDR data representation conversion (NdrConvert, NdrConvert2)
+ *
+ * A call may arrive in the data representation of a peer which is not this machine's: the
+ * integers big-endian, the characters EBCDIC, the floating point numbers in another format.
+ * The representation is named by the four bytes of the format label (rpc_msg.DataRepresentation:
+ * byte 0 holds the character set in its low and the integer byte order in its high nibble, byte
+ * 1 the floating point format), see the "Network Data Representation" chapter of the DCE 1.1
+ * RPC specification (C706).  The stubs call NdrConvert2 before they unmarshal: it walks the
+ * type format strings exactly as the unmarshallers read the buffer and converts the data in
+ * place, so that everything which follows reads a buffer in the local representation.
